WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.600
Industrial systems use several recurring communication patterns.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.460
Recognizing them helps defenders distinguish routine control behavior from meaningful

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:07.900
change.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.110
The patterns are not tied to one protocol,

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.499
and a single site may use several of them at the same time.

00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:17.160
Polling is common when one system repeatedly asks devices for current values or status.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.500
The responding device returns requested data,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.680
creating a stable request-and-response rhythm.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.910
Command-and-response communication is similar,

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.305
but the request may direct an operation and the response confirms acceptance, completion,

00:00:28.305 --> 00:00:29.000
or an error.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.300
Publish-and-subscribe designs separate data producers from consumers.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.890
A publisher makes selected information available,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.300
and subscribers receive updates without individually polling the source.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.100
This can support efficient distribution,

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.640
but defenders still need to understand which publishers, subscribers, topics,

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.048
and data flows are approved.

00:00:45.048 --> 00:00:48.650
Controller-to-controller communication coordinates related equipment, interlocks,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.544
sequencing, or shared process state.

00:00:50.544 --> 00:00:55.295
Supervisory communication connects HMIs or SCADA servers with controllers so operators can

00:00:55.295 --> 00:00:57.800
view conditions and issue authorized actions.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.100
These pathways may be highly repetitive and operationally critical.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.500
Historians collect measurements, alarms, and events for trending, reporting, and analysis.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.100
Their collection paths often cross architectural levels,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.435
so interfaces and replication routes deserve careful design.

00:01:11.435 --> 00:01:14.100
Engineering access is less frequent but more privileged.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.400
It may support configuration, diagnostics, firmware maintenance,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.900
or controller project changes.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.900
A useful baseline describes expected roles, peers, direction, frequency,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.681
and operating windows for each pattern.

00:01:24.681 --> 00:01:27.500
New controller relationships, unexpected publishers,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.800
a historian reaching into an unusual zone,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.785
or engineering access outside maintenance may warrant review.

00:01:32.785 --> 00:01:37.630
The pattern identifies what changed; operators, engineers, and change records explain why.
